pt09 adversarial lane. Surface: intake normalization / cue morphology.

`normalizeText` folds the typographic apostrophe and `tokenize` folds it too,
so retrieval never saw this. The CUE regexes do not: they are matched against
`unit.text`, the reader's own bytes, and every cue spelled with an apostrophe
is written with the ASCII form alone.

Reader-visible, one character apart:

  "Hypergamy means women can't be satisfied with a lower-status partner."
    ASCII  -> "The source wording outruns the matched rule or its uncertainty."
              (Likely source overreach)
    U+2019 -> "The source may identify a boundary or case the indexed rule
              does not yet resolve."   (Possible LE limitation)

The sex-binary overreach rule needs `women` AND `can't`; losing the second
half moves the fault from the claim to the canon.

Prevalence: the archived corpus is majority typographic — 490 curly against
345 straight intra-word apostrophes, 12 of 21 files predominantly curly, 17
curly negation contractions. None of those 17 sits where a cue decides a
label, so the corpus sweep moves 0 of 1,039 segments; the zero is the corpus
being academic prose, not the engine being right.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 tests/lab-analyzer.test.mjs | 60 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 60 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tests/lab-analyzer.test.mjs b/tests/lab-analyzer.test.mjs
index dbed63c..641fd4f 100644
--- a/tests/lab-analyzer.test.mjs
+++ b/tests/lab-analyzer.test.mjs
@@ -2274,3 +2274,63 @@ test('claim detection knows the verb this site is about', async () => {
   // steps are built on.
   assert.equal(unitFor('Issue date 2016 Jan.').isClaimLike, true);
 });
+
+test('a typographic apostrophe is the same word to every cue that reads it', async () => {
+  /*
+   * Red before v2.6.x-opus-pt09 (pt09 adversarial lane, surface: intake
+   * normalization / cue morphology). `normalizeText` folds the typographic
+   * apostrophe and `tokenize` folds it too, so retrieval never saw this. The
+   * CUE regexes do not: they are matched against `unit.text`, which is the
+   * reader’s own bytes, and every cue spelled with an apostrophe —
+   * isn't, aren't, doesn't, don't, can't, won't — is written with the ASCII
+   * form alone. Word, Google Docs and most CMSs emit U+2019, so the cue
+   * simply stopped firing on the majority spelling.
+   *
+   * Measured, one character apart, and reader-visible:
+   *
+   *   "Hypergamy means women can't be satisfied with a lower-status partner."
+   *     ASCII  -> "The source wording outruns the matched rule or its
+   *               uncertainty."            (Likely source overreach)
+   *     U+2019 -> "The source may identify a boundary or case the indexed
+   *               rule does not yet resolve."   (Possible LE limitation)
+   *
+   * The sex-binary overreach rule needs `women` AND `can’t`; losing the
+   * second half moves the fault from the claim to the canon. The Lab tells
+   * the reader their source overreached, or that LE is incomplete, based on
+   * which apostrophe their word processor happened to insert.
+   *
+   * Prevalence: the archived corpus is majority typographic — 490 curly
+   * against 345 straight intra-word apostrophes, 12 of 21 files
+   * predominantly curly, and 17 curly negation contractions. None of those
+   * 17 sits where a cue decides a label, which is why the corpus sweep
+   * moves 0 of 1,039 segments; the zero is the corpus being academic prose,
+   * not the engine being right.
+   */
+  const interpretations = async (text) => {
+    const result = await analyzeDocument(normalizeInput({
+      text, source: { title: 'Apostrophe probe' },
+    }), REAL_CANON);
+    return (result.pressureTests || []).map((entry) => entry.interpretation);
+  };
+
+  const ascii = "Hypergamy means women can't be satisfied with a lower-status partner.";
+  const curly = ascii.replace(/'/g, '’');
+  assert.deepEqual(await interpretations(curly), await interpretations(ascii),
+    'the same sentence, one apostrophe apart, gets the same verdict');
+
+  /*
+   * The source itself, because the defect is not one cue but a habit: seven
+   * regex literals across the engine spelled a contraction with the ASCII
+   * apostrophe alone. A downstream label test can only catch the cue that
+   * happens to be wired to it; this catches the next one written.
+   */
+  const engineSource = readFileSync(new URL('../js/lab-analyzer.js', import.meta.url), 'utf8');
+  const CONTRACTION_IN_REGEX = /\/[^/\n]*[A-Za-z]'[A-Za-z][^/\n]*\/[gimsuy]*/;
+  const asciiOnly = engineSource.split(/\r?\n/)
+    .map((line, index) => [index + 1, line.trim()])
+    .filter(([, line]) => !line.startsWith('*') && !line.startsWith('//'))
+    .filter(([, line]) => CONTRACTION_IN_REGEX.test(line))
+    .map(([number, line]) => `${number}: ${line.slice(0, 90)}`);
+  assert.deepEqual(asciiOnly, [],
+    'a cue regex spells a contraction with the ASCII apostrophe alone');
+});
